    {
      "id": "57de8925-6015-4d79-8545-b32bb967f3cd",
      "phaseId": "0e4c21e5-1cf7-4c0a-a00e-76ffbef60703",
      "number": 4,
      "shortName": "scrolling-is-observable-from-a",
      "title": "Scrolling is observable from a description too — plus the worked example a reader can copy",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "Native code gets `on_scroll*` from T3. A plugin must be able to watch a region it declared, and a\nreader must be able to see what using one looks like end to end.\n\n## Decided with the user (2026-07-26)\n\n`app.on(\"scroll\", ctx)` — the plugin subscribes and the context carries the widget that scrolled and\nits event.\n\n## The tension to settle first (do not skip)\n\nThere are two models in the codebase and this straddles them:\n\n- **Node-level intents** — every other widget event is `.on(\"press\"/\"change\"/\"toggle\", Intent)`,\n  resolved by the host through `dispatch_view_intent`. The intent carries the author's own args, so\n  **identity comes for free**: whatever `arg` the author put on it says which region this is.\n- **The chrome event bus** — `app.on(event, handler)` (heca/src/host.rs) carries app/chrome STATE\n  changes: region resized, pane runtime changed. It is not a widget-input channel today.\n\nPutting widget input on the bus blurs a layer that has been kept clean, and it has an identity\nproblem: `ViewNode` has no node id — only `name`, which exists for form submission — so two scroll\nareas in one panel are indistinguishable to a bus subscriber. Node-level `.on(\"scroll_end\", Intent)`\nanswers identity for free and matches every other event.\n\nRecommended resolution: the node-level intent is the primitive; `app.on(\"scroll\", …)` is a\nconvenience the host can offer on top of it, forwarding to the bus with the node's `name` as the\nidentity. Confirm with the user before building.\n\n## Build\n\n1. Extend the declarative event vocabulary with `scroll_start` / `scroll` / `scroll_end` on\n   `WidgetKind::Scroll` (heca/src/chrome/realize.rs Scroll arm, heca/src/chrome/view.rs props table).\n   The intent args carry the metrics — `offset_y`, `max_y`, etc. — the same way the picker's `change`\n   already carries `value`.\n2. `scroll` fires at frame rate. Marshalling every one to a plugin (and later across the WASM\n   boundary) is not acceptable — either only `scroll_end` crosses, or `scroll` is throttled. Decide\n   and write down which.\n3. The event vocabulary is documented as three verbs (`press`, `change`, `toggle`) in\n   docs/widgets.md and view.rs. Update BOTH, and say why scrolling earns a verb.\n\n## The example (the user asked to see this explicitly)\n\nAdd to docs/widgets.md §ScrollRegion a worked example showing the whole surface in one place:\nmounting a region natively with the three listeners, the same thing as a `ViewNode`, and the one\nline a host needs. It must make obvious that a caller implements NOTHING — no wheel, no thumb, no\nrelease, no track-hold.\n\n## Done when\n\n- A described region can be watched, with an identity its author chose.\n- The rate question has a written answer.\n- docs/widgets.md §ScrollRegion, its declarative note, the props-by-kind tables in both\n  docs/widgets.md and view.rs, and the event-vocabulary paragraph all agree.\n- The USER reads the example and confirms it shows what they asked to see.",
      "notes": "",
